Well, compared to running NetHack under MS-DOS,
you're now running NetHack under an MS-DOS
_simulation_ inside of an MS-Windows base operating
system, a simulation which Microsoft had every
reason to give rotten performance so that its old
MS-DOS products wouldn't look good next to its new
MS-Windows versions of those same products, inciting
customers to upgrade.

I think that alone should explain the loss in
responsiveness, but in addition, MS-Windows seems to
load up immense amounts of other independent process
cruft at startup time before you start running your
first user application, cruft that requires
attention from the swapping of process execution
focus among all those processes. NetHack may well
get the bulk of the execution cycles, but it gets
them with perceptible breaks during which all the
other processes are polled to see if they need some
execution time, and this can and does lead to
visible "hiccups" in NetHack operations like
responding to a keystroke.

At least on my WinXP machine, the disk drive is
rarely idle, and this reflects back in the
slowness of operation of all software.

